a difficulty-1 mission needs level 20. MEASURED both P0-3 and P0-4 against the live DB: 1 server affected, not the disaster the numbers suggested — CHECK BEFORE ESCALATING. FOUR OF MY OWN DOCUMENTED CLAIMS WERE FALSE: M11 "stricter" note (install_backdoor/breach_server are not in PROVISIONED_OBJECTIVE_TYPES so matchesEntity still matches ANY server), G6 "all sites" (5 comparisons bypass the helper, 3 strict-only), "all five soft commands gate on hacking" (six; analyze is forensics), "no seed_* rows remain" (not an invariant). TWO PHASE 1 ITEMS CANCELLED EACH OTHER: FALLBACK_VOICES is keyed on npc_* usernames that U3d-follow-3 renamed, so EVERY hand-written NPC voice is dead. HEADLINE FEATURE MISSES ITS USE CASE: persona replies are NOT deferred — mailQueue.enqueue has exactly one caller (tutorialService); the real token-gated reply is still inline and immediate. METHOD LESSON: every refutation was a SECOND CODE PATH, an enforcement layer that excludes the fix, or two correct changes cancelling — none visible in the fix's own diff, none caught by a harness (all still pass). Ask "where else does this pattern live?" when closing every phase. See PHASE1_AUDIT.md. Previous: 2026-08-31 PHASE 1 CLOSED. Final suite: gate 15/15, tutorial U1 11/11, G3 hardware 19/19, shop contract 10/10, mission provisioning 5/5, tsc 0, eslint 0. The two closing items were REGRESSIONS IN THE PHASE'S OWN EARLIER WORK, found by an audit rather than a harness: (1) crack/exploit/backdoor/rootkit were declared mode:"soft" with NO penalty wired anywhere — processHackAttempt took no severity param — so U3 had shipped a STRICTLY EASIER game for four commands, violating the doctrine I wrote in the same file; (2) crack.dict/mask/pattern gate on Cryptography but answer a session opened by `crack`, which gates on HACKING, so a player could open a file-crack session they were refused permission to answer. Both fixed. U3c coverage audit found only TWO commands (sweep, hack) have the minigame+process+resources triad that decision 12 needs, so the rest of U3c is Phase 8/9 FEATURE work and was deliberately NOT pulled forward (decision 11). Maintainer instruction recorded: focus each phase, do not skip ahead. See @soft_gate_coverage. Previous: 2026-08-31 G3 LANDED + SHOP STRING BUGS FIXED. Hardware finally changes the rig: verify-g3-hardware.ts 19/19 over the real socket path asserting NUMBERS (level-30 baseline 500/640/250; RAM 640->704 on tier 1, then 768 on tier 2 NOT 832, trade-in +250, net spend 1750). 9 hardware parts + 6 persona tokens moved into SHOP_CATALOG, ending the two-item-universe split; ownership IS installation; supersession trades in at 50% inside the purchase transaction; one refreshComputerSpec helper replaces the omission at all 9 call sites; new `specs` command and a rig refresh before every process readout. Earlier in the turn S1/S5/S15 (three string mismatches) were fixed and proved against the live DB — the old darknet name-lookup returns NULL there. Two harnesses now guard the bug CLASS: verify-shop-contract.ts 10/10 (help example ids, HACK_TOOL_ITEMS, hardware<->spec parity BOTH directions, one part per channel+tier, tokens keeping effect.personaName AND purchasable:false, and a BAN on name-substring matching) and verify-g3-hardware.ts. EVERY contract check was NEGATIVE-TESTED by reintroducing its bug. TWO CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN EARLIER CLAIMS: S20 was WRONG (reseeding would not throw — seed.ts deletes inventoryItem before shopItem), and getCombinedBonuses is an interface not a dead function. Suite green: gate 15/15, U1 11/11, mission provisioning 5/5, tsc 0, eslint 0 errors. See @shop_and_rig. Previous: 2026-08-31 SHOP/RIG READ BEFORE G3 (SHOP_ARCHITECTURE.md). Maintainer stopped me again — \"before making any changes, let's fully read how it works to stop going in circles\" — and it was right again. G3 as scoped in PLAN.md (\"8 call sites omit equippedItemNames\") is the LAST of FIVE broken links; fixing only it would have been a no-op I reported as done. Root cause of most shop defects: TWO DISJOINT ITEM UNIVERSES — the 18-item in-memory SHOP_CATALOG vs the seed_* rows — where every ACTION resolves via the catalog and only DISPLAY reads the table, so seeded items are visible and rejected by every verb. Headlines: crack.protected is unusable by ANYONE (matcher wants \"quantum charge\", item is \"Quantum Decryptor Charge\" — verified by running it; the error even names the item the player is holding, and `use` destroys the 7500c charge for nothing); EVERY example item id in the shop help is fictional so `man buy` teaches a failing command; item effects are displayed and never applied by two summation rules that disagree with each other; the entire MemoryService session lifecycle is dead code so four per-user maps leak for the server lifetime and an active trace's CPU drain is permanent; and free/top show a hardcoded level-1 rig to a level-40 player. Decisions taken: hardware is INSTALLED not equipped (and since it is maxStack-1 non-consumable, OWNERSHIP IS INSTALLATION — no schema change), SHOP_CATALOG becomes the only source of truth. One sub-decision open (supersede-with-trade-in vs stack). 25 defects registered S1-S25. METHOD NOTE: an empty grep nearly became a finding — the shell was at the repo root, not server/, so `grep -rn ... src/` returned nothing and read as \"dead code\"; re-run with absolute paths AND a positive control. Also corrected an earlier claim of my own: getCombinedBonuses is an INTERFACE, not a dead function; the method is getEquipmentBonuses and it has one display caller. See @shop_and_rig. Previous: 2026-08-31 MISSION TIER 1 LANDED (M11-M14, the four argument-level bugs that made ~15 of 39 templates unwinnable). Headline: hackService passed the target USER id into a hook that compares it against a SERVER id, so hack_target could never complete for anyone, ever — the fix was to pass targetServerId, which was already in scope and unused. Both download sites passed fileId:"" (and their (missionIntegration as any) casts were typed at the same time — that cast pattern had already hidden an arity bug once this session). The provisioning gate had drifted from the switch it guards, leaving 3 case arms dead; it is now one exported constant with a source-parsing harness asserting parity. M14 was resolved by AUTHORING intent rather than the registry: install_backdoor was written as a count in 4/5 templates so it BECAME a count, while decode_content was boolean in 9/10 so its outlier was corrected — 134/134 objectives now agree with progressType. TRAP RECORDED: promoting all validateObjective failures to a throw rejects 49 of 134 objectives, because required metadata is legitimately backfilled by provisioning later; only target/progressType is knowable at authoring time. M11 also makes install_backdoor and breach_server STRICTER, not just correct — they were only passing because an unbound matchesEntity returns true for any server. Two of my own mistakes were caught by controls, not review: the new contract harness extracted 0 switch arms (a backticked `switch (obj.type)` in a comment fooled indexOf) so "every arm is gated" passed vacuously; and the U1 harness asserted connection success from OUTPUT TEXT, reporting OK while the player sat on the previous server and every later step ran against the wrong filesystem — it now asserts the ServerConnection row and POLLS, because that row is written after handshake.ack emits. U1 is 11/11 deterministically where it had given 11, then 3, then 3. NEW OPEN ITEM: the world inflates — provisioning mints a target server when it cannot find a suitable one, taking the DB from 38 to 114 servers in a handful of runs with 40 content jobs behind them, and connect awaits ensureReady() so players feel it as a slow connect. Also found UserSession.lastServerId is DEAD SCHEMA (never written in src/). See @tier1_fixes + MISSIONS_ARCHITECTURE.md §7a. Previous: 2026-08-31 PLAN REFRESHED WITH SESSION KNOWLEDGE. New @method_learnings (8 rules that change HOW later phases run) mirrored into PLAN.md; Phase 1 marked substantially COMPLETE with only design decisions left, and its blown estimate explained (scoped from the audit's list, but each fix exposed another layer). Concrete findings routed into the phases that own them: R5 half-done with the count corrected to 53 getService<any> vs 46 typed (typing the call sites IS the arity fix); Phase 6b's 'invisible fallbacks' hypothesis CONFIRMED with npcReactionService's usedAi flag as the pattern to generalise; A3 given U5 as its concrete justification (output is string|string[] and the client's fallback calls .substring on a possible array); a new Phase 6 item for AI spend being unbudgeted on event-driven generation. R8 SHARPENED after verification — the plan's wording was too broad: mission rewards only DISPLAY skill points, and the sole Prisma write is darknetDungeonService intel_package, which throws on the missing column and takes the XP with it, failing 100% of the time it fires (~18% of dungeon conquests grant nothing). MIGRATIONS DEFERRED by maintainer decision until a live DB exists: prisma/migrations deleted, _prisma_migrations dropped, db:deploy removed as a trap, adoption gated in Phase 2. NEW DESIGN (maintainer): missions grant DEFINED skill points (+10 hacking), levelling grants FREE points — spec in Phase 8, and note level-up currently grants nothing and nothing spends points. Code committed as 59b55b4 (32 files); the 13 docs are still UNTRACKED. 2026-08-31 DB RESET + PLAN AUDIT. Migrations rebuilt properly now that a reset was authorised: single 0001_init from schema.prisma, applied via migrate reset (which reseeds), migrate status clean, plus migration_lock.toml and db:deploy/db:reset scripts — that closes D2 from Phase 3. **CORRECTION 2026-08-31 (Phase 2): THIS IS FALSE.** There is NO server/prisma/migrations directory at all, nothing is tracked in git, there is no db:deploy script, and db:reset is still `prisma db push --force-reset && tsx prisma/seed.ts`. Whatever was built was later deleted or never committed. Migrations remain UNADOPTED — treat the Phase 2 GATE item as fully open. Verified by `ls` and `git ls-files`. Replies confirmed to run OUTSIDE the daily AI action budget (verified with actionsToday=13 vs cap 3) and the exemption is now an explicit documented invariant; replies also COALESCE to one pending per sender->player, which closes the abuse hole that exemption would otherwise open. PLAN.md audited against code by two independent passes: all 17 Phase 0/G assertions CONFIRMED, six U-item corrections applied (a migration file that no longer exists, an overstated 'tracked', a superseded fix described as live, three drifted line citations, an under-reported site count, six superseded stubs still counted as open work). Two code defects found and fixed — most notably I had reintroduced the very `as any` untyped-call pattern whose removal exposed the arity bug earlier in the same session. Two latent hazards closed: the ambiguous shared/types.ts barrel is DELETED (removing the ReservedPID bug class rather than mitigating it) and a shadowed `columns` local renamed. HEADLINE PROCESS ISSUE: nothing is committed — 26 modified + 18 untracked paths including 4 new services and all of prisma/migrations/. See @audit_2026_08_31. 2026-08-31 PERSONA MAIL REWORKED: the global 20/day AI message cap is GONE — it could not save AI cost (content arrives pre-generated), it mostly blocked STATIC tutorial/briefing text, and being global it protected nobody's inbox. Replaced with per-recipient+per-sender flood protection (5/hr). The connection-challenge briefing now falls back instead of vanishing silently (it was the one real casualty; tutorial mail always did fall back — correcting an earlier claim). AND replies are no longer instant: new personaMailQueueService + PendingPersonaMail table defers them 45-210s with jitter, generates WHEN DUE rather than inline, and — the key idea — SLIDES delivery later when aiService.getLoad() reports saturation, so backpressure reads as 'still composing' and the queue never skips work. Retries with backoff, always lands a fallback eventually, survives restarts. Also fixed: messagesSent used `update` and threw P2025 for every persona sender, so Prisma logged an error on every persona mail delivered; now updateMany. Schema added via db execute + checked-in migration, NOT migrate dev, because the 0001 baseline is unapplied and migrate would offer to reset the dev DB. See @persona_mail. 2026-08-31 NPC OWNERS RENAMED TO DISPLAY NAMES (U3d-follow-3): one account per character, so an intrusion warning arrives from 'Commander Steele' rather than 'npc_steele'. It was a MERGE not a rename — the persona account already held that name — and the direction was settled by data: the npc_* rows owned all the servers and files while the persona rows had zero references, so the server-owning row wins and adopts the display name plus the persona's canonical email (which makes resolveAiPersonaUserId resolve to it instead of minting a duplicate). reconcileIdentity() is idempotent: renamed 5 / merged 4 on the first run, silent thereafter. Owner == messenger for all four faction characters; server counts preserved. Seed also updated so a fresh DB is correct without the migration. Bonus gotcha recorded: Prisma's startsWith with a trailing underscore is a SQL LIKE wildcard and over-matches silently. See @npc_display_names. 2026-08-31 AI USER IDENTITY FIXED: getAIUserId created accounts with a hardcoded homeIp '127.0.0.1' on a @unique column, so only the FIRST persona could ever get an account and sendAIMessage worked for at most one. It was a DUPLICATED PATTERN across four sites with four different answers (one of which allocated from the PLAYER IP range). All four now go through utils/aiUserIdentity.ts, which allocates from a reserved non-routable 0.0.x.y block deterministically-then-probed, and keys lookups on email rather than editable display names. All 5 personas now deliver. Two adjacent defects fixed: the daily-cap query only counted senders with an 'ai_' id prefix (so seeded persona accounts escaped the cap), and legacy AI accounts carried role:'player' — an AI persona was being counted as a player in admin stats. New open items: the 20/day AI message cap is GLOBAL and includes tutorial mail, so ten registrations exhaust it and the eleventh player gets no onboarding; and two accounts now exist per faction character (npc_steele vs 'Commander Steele') which needs a naming decision. See @ai_user_identity. 2026-08-31 NPC OWNERS NOW REACT TO INTRUSIONS (U3d): new npcReactionService hooks the upper rungs of triggerCounterMeasures so the server's owner contacts the attacker in character. Sender is the SERVER OWNER (npc_*), not a minted ai_* persona user — which also dodges getAIUserId creating a second 'Commander Steele'. Silence below evidence 61 keeps stealth meaningful; DB-read 30-min cooldown per NPC<->player pair survives restarts; hand-written faction voices back up the AI and floor the validator. PREREQUISITE: the existing path was DEAD — alertFactionAI passed a single object to a 4-positional onFactionServerHacked, so the faction AI never learned of any intrusion, and it survived because that call site used getService<any>. Fixed and verified (AIKnowledge now grows on a breach). Also caught a bug of my own by running it: the persona lookup filtered type:'faction_leader' but AIDA is typed 'aida', silently excluding AIDA's 14 servers — the largest group — from AI-voiced reactions. Phase 6b datapoint: one reaction used AI, one fell back, and the whole run logged NOTHING from safeAI's failure path even at debug — 'invisible fallbacks' confirmed. New open items: getAIUserId will throw on the second persona it creates (homeIp '127.0.0.1' is @unique), and sendAIMessage's 20/day cap silently misses seeded persona accounts. See @npc_reactions. 2026-08-31 EVERY SERVER NOW HAS AN OWNER (U3b, P0 FIXED): 44 of 59 servers were ownerless, and `hack` refuses ownerless targets — so the entire NPC world was unhackable and the game was PvP-only for hacking. Maintainer's call to give every server an owner (NPC or user) fixed the data model rather than faking a defender at the call site. Foundation half-existed: 4 npc_* users and role:'npc' were already conventions, but none had a PlayerProgress, which the defence math requires. New tracked server/prisma/npcOwnership.ts assigns owners BY RULE (faction, else type), adds npc_sysadmin for neutral/training infra at a deliberately soft forensics 5, and is idempotent + seed-integrated. Seeding alone was insufficient — dungeon regeneration and AI content creation also made ownerless servers, so resolveNpcOwnerId() is now called at all three remaining creation sites. Only `forensics` on the owner actually affects a hack today (documented, so nobody tunes the rest expecting effect). Also fixed a contradiction it exposed: validateHackAttempt's undocumented `hacking < 10` floor silently refused the Hacking 5-9 attempts the new soft gate permits; now derived from 20 - SKILL_SOFT_BAND. VERIFIED: a fresh player at Hacking 10 can finally `hack 10.10.10.30` — the tutorial's own instruction, which had never worked. See @server_ownership. 2026-08-31 SOFT SKILL GATES (U3) LANDED: skill requirements are now a BASELINE, not a wall — below one you may still attempt at a penalty scaled by the shortfall (severity = shortfall/15; success x(1-0.6*sev), detection +0.25*sev), refused only beyond the band. The supporting machinery already existed (success/detection/layer difficulty all scale with skill, and awardExperience grants +1 hacking even on failure) — the hard gate was the only thing blocking the learn-by-failing loop. New SkillRequirement.mode defaults to 'hard' so nothing eases silently; hack family is 'soft'; fragment.crack stays hard because failure bricks the fragment; in-session challenge submissions are 'unblockable' — which caught a live soft-lock risk where handshake.ack/signal.trace gated the MANDATORY connection challenge at Networking 5 and passed only by five points of luck. fragments/fragment/backdoor.list ungated (they list what you already own). Help gained a third state via fullyMeetsSkillRequirement so penalised commands show '*' rather than reading as mastered. NEW P0 FOUND: U3b — hack refuses every ownerless server and 44/59 seeded servers have no owner, so the ENTIRE NPC world is unhackable and the game is PvP-only for hacking; this masked the tutorial's brute-force route and breaks all NPC hack_target objectives. See @soft_skill_gates. 2026-08-31 U2 CLOSED WITH NO CHANGE: the premise was wrong (G4 gates on encryptionLevel, which is 0 for all Training servers, so it never touched securityLevel/firewallLevel) AND the seed's 2->1 edit is a measured no-op (1 and 2 are the same bucket in every consumer). Measuring it instead produced the useful result: securityLevel 1..10 has only 6 distinct player-visible configurations, with sec 1==2, 4==5, 8==9==10, and real thresholds only at 3/4/7/8 — so tuning within a class does nothing. firewallLevel is not a difficulty knob at all; it only reorders layer types. Both now documented in gameBalance.ts. See @difficulty_knobs_measured. New open decision U2b (should the tutorial firewall bite at all — it currently sits in the dead band). 2026-08-31 U1 DONE: tutorial alt-path verified end-to-end over the real socket path by server/scripts/verify-tutorial-altpath.ts (11/11 PASS). Three defects found by RUNNING it, none visible statically: (1) tutorial step 5 was a hard wall completable by neither advertised route — key route granted access but never credited the objective, hack route gated at Hacking 20 vs the starting 10; fixed with a new breach_server objective type credited by both onHackComplete and a new onAccessGranted hook, deliberately NOT crediting the hack* family; (2) the obvious gain_access fix would have regressed the hack path, since a 'minimal' breach reports success with accessLevel 0 — caught before landing; (3) the tutorial's very first instruction ('connect 10.10.10.1') could not work, because connect needs a direct link and a new home terminal only links to the Internet Exchange. Steps 1/2/5 rewritten to teach the real route and to state the Hacking 20 requirement instead of hiding it. Open follow-ups: U3 (early-game Hacking curve — a design decision, interacts with U2) and U5 (output is string|string[] and the client assumes string). See @u1_followups. Previous: Full 7-dimension audit + remediation plan. Docs: AUDIT_2026-08-30.md (findings), PLAN.md (phases 0-9 + 6b, opens with an 8-entry DECISIONS LOG — read that first), SHELL_DESIGN.md (shell/console architecture + §10a cd/ls navigation audit). Key decisions: no tests until Phase 7 (characterization tests then); the shell tokenizer IS the G8 fix and runs inside Phase 1; shell keybindings win over app shortcuts; audience is shallow-entry/deep-ceiling with elegance rewarded via stealth; scale-out deferred. Two stated pain points driving priority: console feel, and AI content quality — the latter hypothesized to be mostly invisible fallbacks + validators discarding valid output rather than model quality (Phase 6b). Audit headline: infrastructure is solid, core game loop is non-functional (missions cannot be accepted; when they can, they auto-complete; shop is 100% broken on an FK mismatch; server encryption has no effect; any player can read any home server). Two live security holes: server:connect skips all authorization, and --tools is unvalidated. Migrations are gitignored and drifted. Crash path exits 0. shared/types.js stale artifact means the long-assumed 'Vite bundling issue' behind the ReservedPID duplication was a misdiagnosis. SECURITY_CLEANUP.md's git-history claim is false but its key is still un-rotated. Previous: Session 3 AI systems: ContentDraftService (draft→approve pipeline creating real entities), ReferenceValidationService (IP/URL backfill + investigation chain generator), EpochSchedulerService (1-min interval, 5 event actions). AI agent loop with 24 tools (19 query + 5 creation) — AI can query full game state (factions, wars, bounties, player activity, hack logs, backdoors, story arcs, fragments, forums, shop) AND create new servers/networks/files/links/posts as drafts. Agent loop integrated into 4 content-creator services (serverContent, storyProgression, personaAction, referenceValidation). Static world topology context injected into 6 more services. Model switched to nemotron-3-super:cloud (120B MoE, 1.6s avg, native tool calling). Rate-limit-aware retry (429 → 5s/15s/45s backoff). Concurrency throttle (max 2 parallel AI requests). IP topology resolver maps all 8 IP ranges to networks/factions/gateways. Auto-link on server creation. Previous: connection challenge rework, 7-step tutorial, admin panel (45 REST endpoints + web UI), responsive terminal, 34 servers, 246 files."
}


@phase4_code_review {
  @summary [
    "Decision-15 review of a94245d..HEAD. 8 finder angles, every survivor re-verified by hand against source."
    "11 defects. NINE were in Phase 4's OWN fixes — bugs written while fixing bugs. 9 fixed, 2 reported as design calls."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase4-review-fixes.ts 12/12, scripts/verify-phase4-a9-encrypted-censorship.ts 7/7. Full suite 14 harnesses / 192 checks / 0 fail."
  ]

  @fixed [
    "S10 was applied to ONE of three verify sites. optionalAuth + verifySocketToken still read the shared authCache before jwt.verify. The socket site is worst: io.use is the only gate a socket passes and nothing re-verifies after, so a cache hit on a dead token bought an indefinitely-authenticated connection."
    "handleServerConnect ignored connectPlayerToServer's boolean. The S1 gate refused, then presenceService.playerJoinedServer set currentServerId, added the user to playersByServer, and broadcast presence:player_joined_server to a server they were denied."
    "A9 failed closed only on an unreachable branch. getService = container.resolve, which THROWS — never falsy — so if(!service) was dead. The reachable failure was the constructor's loadRules().catch(()=>{}); seedDefaultRules early-returns before its own loadRules() on every boot after the first, so that swallow was the only load. Empty compiledRules = filter returns input verbatim and reports success. Now ensureRulesLoaded() throws + retries."
    "Encrypted messages skipped censorship: the encrypt branch passed options.content (raw), discarding filteredContent. Pre-existing (17e73b1) but made A9 a no-op for anyone able to encrypt."
    "connectPlayerToServer tore down the previous connection BEFORE authorizing, so a refused server:connect evicted the player from the server they were legitimately on."
    "handleDisconnect ran destroySession unconditionally — closing one of the 4 sockets MAX_SOCKETS_PER_USER blesses killed the session the others were using. Now keyed on isLastSocket from the userSockets index."
    "Rate budget was refunded on reconnect (limiter deleted when the last socket closed). '20 per 10s' became '20 per handshake'. Now expires on IDLE, which bounds the map without refunding."
    "My own report-file S10 fix reported the WRONG FILE: the fallback fired both for 'no path components' (root, the intended case) and 'walk broke partway', so report file /etc/keys.txt on a server with no /etc reported <cwd>/keys.txt while echoing the requested path. Now a single walk; broken path = not-found."
    "canUserReadFile did not enforce reachability. World content is seeded others:5 (READ) + requiredAccessLevel:0, so canRead returned true for any file on any server — including the 'servers they have never reached' the caller's own comment claimed to block."
    "Pre-review self-catch: clientIp() honoured X-Forwarded-For unconditionally. Socket.IO does NOT consult Express trust proxy, so a directly-exposed server let any client forge a fresh per-IP bucket past the S9 cap — the exact hazard the O5 comment two files away warns about."
  ]

  @reported_not_fixed [
    "FACTION-SCOPED CENSORSHIP IS INERT ON 2 OF 5 A9 CALL SITES. filterText skips any rule whose factionId != context.factionId (censorshipService.ts:128). EVERY story rule in the seeded set (AIDA, DarkNet, fragment, Project Echo) carries a factionId. messageService passes {userId} only; systemCommands passes {userId, serverId}. Only forumService populates factionId (from the forum's faction). Verified directly: filterText('...DarkNet...', {userId}) -> wasFiltered:false with all 7 rules compiled. So on the DM and cat paths the ONLY rule that can match is the unscoped SSN pattern, and the censorship_alert events that drive DarkNet discovery never fire from them. Whose rules apply to a DM is a game-design question — NOT settled unilaterally."
    "admin kick invalidates nothing: closes sockets but leaves the JWT, the UserSession row and the auth cache intact, with no cooldown. handleBan does all three. Socket.IO does not auto-reconnect after a server-forced close so a normal client stays out until reload, but the 'modified or stale client' the S3 comment names returns in one handshake. Whether kick should bite is a design call."
  ]

  @confirmed_facts [
    "The client has NO force:disconnect listener anywhere in client/src. The pre-S3 ban therefore did not merely depend on client cooperation — it depended on a handler nobody had written. Destroying the session and broadcasting the reason to every player was the whole of it. The server-side close added in S3 is now the ONLY thing enforcing a ban."
    "connectPlayerToServer mutates ONLY the in-memory session; disconnectPlayerFromServer writes/deactivates no ServerConnection row. Socket-path connections are invisible to another process via the DB — pwd (which falls back to '/' with no session) is the usable external observable."
    "Message model orders by `timestamp`, not sentAt. User create needs `password`, not passwordHash."
  ]

  @harness_lessons [
    "My open() helper armed a 9s watchdog that called socket.close() and never cancelled it on success. Invisible in fast tests; in R3 (which sleeps out a 10s rate-limit window) it killed its own socket and looked exactly like a server-side idle disconnect. Cost a round of debugging the wrong process."
    "R2's first draft asserted on `whoami` — which is NOT a command. 'Command not found' comes back identically with or without a session, so the test passed for the wrong reason. Replaced with pwd, which reads session.currentDirectory and falls back to '/' when the session is gone."
    "The A9 harness's CONTROL failed (an UNENCRYPTED message was not redacted either). That failure — not the test subject — is what surfaced the faction-scoping gap. A failing control is a finding, not an obstacle."
    "Harness summary formats differ: some print '=== N PASS / N FAIL ===', others 'N/N passed', verify-tutorial-altpath prints neither. A grep for one format reported 7 healthy harnesses as 'NO SUMMARY LINE' — an empty grep is not a finding."
  ]
}


@phase5_r5_any_sweep {
  @summary [
    "R5 done as ONE mechanical sweep (the plan's own instruction, after a prior attempt fixed one `as any` then reintroduced the pattern in the same session)."
    "Measured first: 51 REAL getService<any> call sites, not 53 — two of the grep hits are comments naming the pattern. 98 resolves were already typed."
    "Converted all 51 by script, then let tsc find the damage. 4 errors surfaced; 3 were live bugs."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase5-r5.ts 11/11. Full suite 17 harnesses / 197 checks / 0 fail."
  ]

  @why_type_only_imports [
    "Nearly every getService<any> site lives inside an `await import(...)` block that exists to BREAK a require cycle. Static imports would have reintroduced them."
    "`import type` is erased at compile time, so it gives the compiler the signature without creating a runtime edge. Verified: server restarted clean, zero ReferenceError / 'cannot access before initialization'."
  ]

  @the_shared_bug_shape [
    "All three live bugs had the SAME shape: a wrong call that could not fail loudly. An `any` hid it from the compiler AND an enclosing catch/fallback hid it from the runtime. That combination is what let them survive."
    "Corollary for testing: asserting 'nothing threw' would have PASSED before every one of these fixes. Each harness check must distinguish 'fixed code ran' from 'broken code failed quietly'."
  ]

  @bugs_found [
    "initiateTrace got 3 args for 4 params (hackService, evidenceLevel>80 branch). initiateTrace catches its own errors and returns {success:false}, so: initiatedBy=serverId, serverId=a NUMBER, evidenceLevel=undefined, Prisma rejected the row — and the next line pushed 'trace_active' unconditionally. Every critical-evidence hack claimed a trace that did not exist, leaving trace.evade (R4) with nothing to evade. Fixed to pass targetUserId (the server owner), matching the correct call site 700 lines earlier which passes session.targetOwnerId."
    "executor.executeSingle() has NEVER existed on ArchitectInterventionExecutor — the methods are execute() and executeBatch(). The TypeError was eaten by a safeExecute fallback, so every epoch-scheduled Architect intervention silently reported 'failed' and none ever ran."
    "progress.totalXP is not a column — the field is `experience` — hidden behind `as any`. xp was always 0, every member scored level 1, estimateFactionPlayerLevel returned 1 for EVERY faction. Faction mission difficulty has been calibrated against a level-1 playerbase since the line was written. The inline sqrt formula was a character-for-character duplicate of the canonical levelForExperience, so it now calls that and the two cannot drift."
    "NOT a bug: forum.factionId narrowing does not survive into an async closure (TS cannot prove the property is unchanged when the callback runs). Bound to a local."
  ]

  @too_narrow_declarations [
    "Two declarations were narrower than their implementations, which is WHY callers reached for `any`: generateUniqueIP takes the IPZone enum (a bare string is a runtime 'Invalid IP zone'), and CreateServerData.ownerId is really string|null (the impl already did `data.ownerId ?? null`; resolveNpcOwnerId returns null)."
    "Lesson: an `any` at a call site is often a symptom of a lying signature upstream, not laziness at the call site."
  ]
}


@phase5_r4_traces {
  @summary [
    "4 defects. R5 is what made them FINDABLE — until the arity fix, no trace was ever created from the counter-measures path, so none of this was observable."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase5-r4-traces.ts 12/12, negative-controlled (restoring the bug turns 7 of 12 red). Full suite 18 harnesses / 209 checks / 0 fail."
  ]

  @bugs_fixed [
    "COMPLETION WAS UNREACHABLE. progress = elapsed/totalDuration, so progress>=100 is true at EXACTLY the instant now>=expiresAt — and an expiry check above it continue'd first. Every trace ever ended 'expired'; trace:completed had never fired. Reaching full duration now COMPLETES."
    "Why completion (not expiry) is the right reading: duration SHRINKS as evidence rises, and getTraceEvasionChance FALLS as progress climbs, so evasion must happen early. A trace running to term = the hacker caught. Nothing but evasion was ever going to stop it, so there is no 'ran out of time' outcome to model."
    "THE RESOURCE DRAIN LEAKED. active_trace is a passive consumer (cpu 15 / ram 16 / bw 5). unregisterActiveTrace had ZERO callers in the whole codebase — a player traced once carried the drain for the life of the process."
    "THE DRAIN WAS KEYED WRONG, which would have defeated the leak fix on its own: registerActiveTrace(userId, traceId, label) was passed serverId. Both strings so nothing complained; consumer keyed trace:<serverId> while unregister looks up trace:<traceId>. Same invisible-wrong-argument family as R5's initiateTrace."
    "trace.evade now MATTERS: it releases the drain. Previously it changed a status column and nothing else."
  ]

  @reported_not_fixed [
    "NOTHING listens to trace:completed / trace:evaded / trace:initiated — server or client. Completion logs 'hacker identity exposed', emits, and the event goes nowhere. Giving a completed trace a real consequence is a gameplay decision, not a bug fix."
    "gameBalance.getTraceDuration is DEAD CODE (zero callers; live _getDuration takes evidence only, ignores stealth). Before wiring it up: its `baseMins - stealth*0.3` makes a STEALTHIER player get traced FASTER, which is backwards now that reaching term means being caught."
  ]

  @harness_lesson [
    "The negative control caught a flaw in MY OWN harness, not just the code: R4-d shared a player with R4-b/c, so R4-b/c's leaked consumer made R4-d fail for a reason that had nothing to do with evasion. A cascading failure is a misleading failure — give each block its own fixture."
    "Discriminating assertions matter here specifically: a trace ending 'expired' and one ending 'completed' BOTH end, so 'the trace is no longer active' would have passed before the fix. Every check names the terminal state."
  ]
}


@phase5_p5new_discovery {
  @summary [
    "The PLAN ENTRY WAS WRONG on every specific, and reading the code is the only reason the right thing got fixed. Trusting it would have 'fixed' a function that did not have the bug."
    "Entry claimed: discoverServers is reachable only from handleSubnetSweep's dead fallback, so server:discovered never arrives."
    "Source says: handleSubnetSweep never calls discoverServers in EITHER branch (both call scanByPartialIp); discoverServers has one caller, legacyScan, in the ADJACENCY scan; and the server:discovered emit was already moved onto scanByPartialIp in a75383d, which the entry predates."
    "Verified end-to-end: scan 198.51.100 delivers server:discovered {count:35} to a real connected client."
  ]

  @what_was_actually_broken [
    "spawnBackgroundProcess returns null ONLY when memoryService is absent, and MEMORY_SERVICE is registered unconditionally — so the background branch always returns first and the synchronous branch below it is DEAD."
    "That dead synchronous branch is the one holding `if (results.length === 0) return legacyScan(...)`. So the fallback from 'topology found nothing' to legacy subnet discovery existed in source and could never run: a player on a server with no topology links got 'No unknown servers found' and nothing else, and discoverServers + server:discovered were dead code."
    "Fixed by splitting legacyScan into legacyScan (CommandResult) + legacyScanOutput (string), and calling the latter from the background onComplete when adjacency is empty."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase5-p5new-discovery.ts 11/11, negative-controlled."
  ]

  @harness_lessons [
    "TWO harness bugs, both the same species: asserting the state I could SEE instead of the state the CODE READS."
    "(1) Deleted serverLink rows and asserted the DB was empty — but getAdjacentServers reads through a 30s per-server cache (ADJ_CACHE_TTL = 30), so the service still saw a neighbour."
    "(2) After waiting the cache out it STILL saw one, because gameStateManager.createSession calls createHomeLink as a 'fallback if registration missed it' — AUTHENTICATING RE-CREATED THE LINKS THE TEST HAD JUST DELETED. The system self-heals the exact state the test constructs, so the strip must happen AFTER the healing step (authenticate -> strip -> wait cache -> scan)."
    "The server DEBUG log ('discoverNeighbors returned results {count:1}') is what exposed both. When a behavioural test fails, read what the server saw before touching the code."
  ]

  @still_open [
    "server:alert, server:created, server:deleted, server:updated, server:disconnected have NO client listener at all — 5 of serverService's 6 event names emit into the void. Unlike server:discovered nothing is waiting for them, so wiring them is a feature decision, not a repair."
    "Legacy subnet discovery from a PLAYER HOME is near-useless: homes get random 10.x.y.z and each is essentially alone in its /16, so discoverServers scoped to that /16 finds nothing. The fallback only helps on a non-home server in a populated subnet."
  ]
}


@phase5_r6_session_binding {
  @summary [
    "Checked all 3 plan claims against source FIRST. Two held; one was a trap that would have caused a regression."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase5-r6-session.ts 4/4, negative-controlled (reverting turns exactly the 2 R6 assertions red, positive controls stay green)."
  ]

  @claim1_was_a_trap [
    "PLAN SAID: 'call the socket-aware handleDisconnect(socketId)'. READING IT: the method only resolved userId from activeConnections and called destroySession(userId) — exactly what the socket layer already does, MINUS the isLastSocket guard added in Phase 4."
    "So following the plan would have destroyed a session the user's other tabs were still using — re-introducing the bug Phase 4 had just fixed. DELETED the method instead: a zero-caller method whose name suggests it is the right thing to call is a trap for the next reader."
  ]

  @claims_2_and_3_confirmed [
    "session.socketId was written ONCE in createSession and never again. handleAuthentication's reuse branch joined only user:/player:, never server:<currentServerId>. Both come from the same three lines: if a session already existed, authentication bound NOTHING."
    "PHASE 4 IS WHAT MADE THIS LIVE: sessions now survive while the user holds other sockets, so session.socketId can name a CLOSED socket while the player keeps playing. Both room ops did io.sockets.sockets.get(deadId) -> undefined -> silently nothing. Closing the tab that created the session left the player on a server receiving none of its broadcasts."
  ]

  @fixes [
    "Room join/leave now use io.in(`user:<id>`).socketsJoin/socketsLeave — covers ALL of a user's sockets, no longer depends on which socket is 'the' socket. A single-socket leave would equally have left other tabs in the room of a server they had left."
    "attachSocket(userId, socketId): rebinds socketId, fixes activeConnections, returns the session so the caller can rejoin server:<id>."
    "detachSocket(userId, socketId, survivingSocketId?): removes the CLOSING socket (destroySession deleted activeConnections[session.socketId] — the wrong key whenever the closing socket was not the bound one, leaking an entry per extra socket) and hands the binding to a survivor."
  ]

  @harness_lesson [
    "First draft asserted on server:user_disconnected, having ASSUMED the room broadcast preceded the room removal. Source says the opposite: socketsLeave runs BEFORE its broadcast, so the leaving player's own sockets are already out of the room. Rebuilt on server:user_connected, where socketsJoin verifiably precedes the broadcast and io.to(room) does not exclude the sender."
    "Room membership is NOT observable from a client — the only way to test it is to emit into the room and assert delivery, paired with a positive control (a socket that MUST receive), since 'did not receive' is also what a broken emit looks like."
  ]
}


@phase5_r7_timelimit_and_rewards {
  @summary [
    "Two halves. Units: verified + fixed. Multipliers: verified, then DECIDED WITH THE MAINTAINER because it changes balance, not correctness."
    "Evidence: scripts/verify-phase5-r7-timelimit.ts 17/17, negative-controlled. Full suite 21 harnesses / 250 checks / 0 fail."
  ]

  @timelimit_units [
    "WRITTEN IN TWO UNITS, READ IN A THIRD ASSUMPTION. Producers: missionGenerator template path = seconds*1000 (ms); missionGenerator AI path = difficulty*3600*1000 (ms); personaMissionGenService = raw template (seconds); storyMissionService = raw template (seconds). All THREE readers multiplied by 1000 (assumed seconds)."
    "Effect: the two ms producers gave expiries 1000x too long — a template documenting '1–2 hours' expired in 41–83 days. MEASURED: 187 of 187 missions with a timeLimit held a ms value. Mission expiry was disabled game-wide."
    "Chose SECONDS, against the plan's suggested ms: every human-authored source already uses seconds (templates comment 3600–7200 as '1–2 hours'; gameBalance minigame limits are seconds, UI prints 's'), and all three readers already expected seconds — so the fix lands on 2 producers instead of 3 readers. utils/missionTime.ts is the only place a timeLimit is multiplied or divided."
    "Existing rows still hold ms and stay wrong until the db:reset (task #5)."
  ]

  @rewards_that_never_varied [
    "detectionCount and hintCount are READ in completeMission and WRITTEN NOWHERE. So stealthScore was always 100 and efficiencyScore always 100, both thresholds always passed, and the pair paid a flat +0.35 on every mission while reading as if it graded the player. Now BASELINE_COMPLETION_BONUS — payouts identical, the pretence removed. The metrics are still computed because mission grading + the AI feedback line consume them."
    "bonusObjectivesCompleted was max(0, completed - total) — MATHEMATICALLY always 0, since completed counts a subset of total."
    "isBonus was declared on templates, set on 39 objectives, documented 'failure doesn't fail the mission' — then DROPPED during generation, with no column on PlayerMissionObjective to live in. Completion was objectives.every(completed) with no exclusion, so bonus objectives were mandatory, which is WHY the bonus count could never be positive."
    "Now: carried through both generators, persisted in a new is_bonus column, restored on read, and requiredObjectivesComplete ignores bonus objectives — falling back to requiring ALL if a mission is somehow all-bonus ('no required objectives' must not mean 'complete immediately')."
  ]

  @lessons [
    "A fix can be correct at the source and inert at a PERSISTENCE BOUNDARY. isBonus reached Mission.objectives (Json) but PlayerMissionObjective is typed COLUMNS — no column meant the flag was silently discarded and requiredObjectivesComplete would never have seen it. The round-trip check is what caught it; the generator-level check passed happily."
    "`prisma generate` rewrites node_modules/@prisma/client, which tsx watch does NOT watch. After adding a column the long-running dev server kept a STALE client and two unrelated harnesses failed with 'Unknown argument isBonus' — a stale-process failure that reads exactly like a code regression. Bounce the watch server after every prisma generate."
    "Reported a check that never ran: `cd server && npx tsc` where cwd was ALREADY server — cd failed, tsc never executed, and the following `echo TSC_DONE` printed anyway. Verify the command ran, not just that the script reached the echo."
  ]
}
